diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 20f756e..dfe7c62 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -4,6 +4,57 @@
 
 ### Added
 
+- **`VISAGE_REJECTED_DIR`: keep the faces that failed to authenticate.** Off by
+  default. When set, every verify that saw a face and matched no enrolled model stores
+  the frame that was judged as a grayscale PNG named
+  `<UTC time>_<user>_<similarity>.png`, so the owner can see who tried to unlock their
+  machine. Verifies that saw no face store nothing. The files are biometric captures of
+  whoever was in front of the camera: directory `0700`, files `0600`, and only the
+  newest `VISAGE_REJECTED_KEEP` (default 100) are kept. Also
+  `services.visage.rejectedFaces.{dir,keep}` on NixOS and `rejected_dir` in
+  `visage status`.
+
+- **`VISAGE_FACE_WAIT_MS`: keep looking for a face instead of giving up after one burst.**
+  A verify starts the moment authentication is requested, which is often before the
+  user is in front of the camera: the lid is still opening, or the sensor is still
+  dark after resume. Until now that verify took one 3-frame burst, found no face and
+  failed, and the user got the password prompt while looking straight at the camera.
+
+  With a window set, a burst that sees no face (or only dark frames) is followed by
+  another until the window or the verify timeout runs out. A burst that **finds** a
+  face ends the verify exactly as before, matched or not: the window never turns one
+  request into several attempts against the gallery, which the per-request rate
+  limiter would not see.
+
+  Default `0` keeps the single-burst behaviour. Keep the window below the client's
+  D-Bus timeout (`pam_visage.so timeout=N`, default 3 s); once PAM has given up,
+  further bursts only keep the camera busy. Also `services.visage.faceWaitMs` on
+  NixOS, `face_wait_ms` in `visage status`, and `bursts` in the verify timing line.
+
+- **Optional GPU inference through OpenVINO (`openvino` cargo feature,
+  `VISAGE_INFERENCE_DEVICE=gpu`).** Both models run on the GPU instead of ONNX Runtime
+  on the CPU. The default build and the default setting are unchanged.
+
+  The GPU path cannot take face authentication down. OpenVINO is loaded at runtime
+  rather than linked, so a build with the feature still starts where it is not
+  installed; and if the runtime, its GPU plugin or the GPU itself is missing, or a
+  model fails to compile, loading logs a warning and falls back to the CPU.
+
+  Compiled GPU kernels are cached in `VISAGE_INFERENCE_CACHE_DIR` (default `gpu-cache`
+  beside the model directory). Without the cache every daemon start, including the
+  restart after each resume, recompiles both models, which takes seconds on an
+  integrated GPU.
+
+  The packaged unit is unchanged: GPU users add `DeviceAllow=char-drm rw` with
+  `systemctl edit visaged`, so nobody else's sandbox grows. On NixOS,
+  `services.visage.inference.device = "gpu"` sets both.
+
+- **`VISAGE_CPU_THREADS`: ONNX Runtime threads per model**, previously hard-coded to 2.
+  The default is unchanged. On a 4-core, 8-thread i7-1160G7 (plugged in, balanced
+  profile), 4 threads took SCRFD from about 165 to 125 ms and ArcFace from about 135 to
+  100 ms per frame. Also `services.visage.inference.cpuThreads` on NixOS and
+  `cpu_threads` in `visage status`.
+
 - **A framing phase before each enrollment capture.** The user gets a moment to see
   themselves and get centred before anything is captured, instead of being told to
   look at a lens and hoping.
@@ -80,6 +131,36 @@
   This is the test that was missing when `framingMs` went unexposed: a missing option is
   not a build error, not a warning, and not visible in `visage status`.
 
+### Changed
+
+- **A verify examines frames as they arrive, on one camera stream.** Every capture
+  burst used to open a new V4L2 stream and wait for all of its frames before examining
+  any. Starting the stream is most of a capture on UVC IR modules: ~320 ms from
+  `STREAMON` to the first frame on a Syntek `174f:118f`, against ~35 ms between frames.
+  A verify now runs one stream for its whole length, including the face-wait window,
+  and stops at the first frame that settles it. On a ThinkPad X12 a verify in view went
+  from ~400 to ~335 ms, and the face-wait window examines a frame every ~40 ms instead
+  of restarting the camera for every burst. The decision rules are unchanged; a burst is
+  now a group of `frames_per_verify` usable frames within the stream (`bursts` in the
+  timing line counts groups). `Camera::stream_frames` is the new entry point; the
+  capture loop's observer may now end it early.
+
+  "No usable frames" is reported only when every frame of the verify was dark, so a
+  verify that saw frames and then went dark no longer counts toward the camera
+  self-heal.
+
+- **A successful verify no longer runs ArcFace on frames after the first match.** The
+  outcome is a match if any frame matches, so once one has, the remaining frames'
+  extractions can only change the reported similarity, never the decision. That was
+  two of three extractions per successful verify at the default
+  `frames_per_verify=3`, the waste `extract_n` in the timing line was added to expose.
+  With liveness on, the remaining frames are still run through detection, because the
+  landmark check needs every frame and fails closed without them; with liveness off
+  they are skipped entirely.
+
+  The reported similarity is now that of the first matching frame rather than the
+  best one. Failed verifies are unchanged: every frame is still examined.
+
